WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:08.700
Good Morning Hank.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.870
It's Wednesday, January 30th and today's video
comes to you in one part.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:14.139
Part one: I am not a Pornographer.

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:18.150
Hank, you may remember that almost three years
ago my first novel, Looking for Alaska, was

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.150
published.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:22.500
I just found out that Looking for Alaska is
about to be taught to 11th grade english students

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.210
at Depew High school, outside of Buffalo,
New York.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.250
Alaska is taught in a lot of English classes
around the country, but it's still pretty

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.940
awesome to hear that someone is gonna like,
teach your book in a regular english class,

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.660
and people are gonna read it closely and think
hard about it.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:35.840
So here's what happened.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.320
Because there are a few scenes in Looking
for Alaska that some people find controversial,

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.770
the Depew High School administrators and English
teachers got together, and they wrote a letter

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.380
saying, 'We're gonna teach this book.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:46.490
We think it's pretty good.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:50.370
If you're okay with your kid being taught
this book, please sign this permission slip,

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:52.010
otherwise the kid will read some other book.'

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.530
So parents who are cool with their kids reading
Looking for Alaska get to have their kids

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:59.079
read Looking for Alaska, and parents who aren't
cool with it get to have their kids read some

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:00.079
other book.

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:02.540
But there were a few people who weren't happy
with this solution.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.049
These people didn't actually have kids who
are in the 11th grade... but no matter.

00:01:06.049 --> 00:01:09.960
They think my book is pornographic and that
it will cause immoral thoughts and actions

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:10.960
in children.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.070
These people believe that no one should be
allowed to read the book, even those people

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.569
whose parents signed the permission slip.

00:01:16.569 --> 00:01:17.719
*Grumbles*

00:01:17.719 --> 00:01:22.130
Now Hank, it's no secret that I like for things
to be about me, but this really isn't.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.289
Still I have to tell you that when you're
told that the novel that you wrote for teenagers

00:01:25.289 --> 00:01:29.659
is pornography, you start to feel a little
bit, mmm... mad.

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:32.310
I don't know exactly what it is that bothers
me about that characterization.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.280
I mean, it's not the -ography.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.899
I wouldn't be mad if someone was like, 'We
must get this book out of our classrooms;

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:39.569
it's full of geography!'

00:01:39.569 --> 00:01:44.340
Or, 'No teenager should ever have to read
this disgusting work of lexicography.'

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.179
No Hank, it's not the -graphy that bothers
me.

00:01:47.179 --> 00:01:48.279
It's the porn.

00:01:48.279 --> 00:01:49.939
Pornography is designed to titillate.

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:54.109
Hank, I don't think there's a single halfway-normal
person in the world who would find a single

00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:56.849
thing in my book in anyway arousing.

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:58.869
There is one very frank sex scene.

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:03.749
It is awkward, un-fun, disastrous, and wholly
unerotic.

00:02:03.749 --> 00:02:07.359
Hank the whole reason that scene in question
exists in Looking for Alaska is because I

00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:11.660
wanted to draw contrast between that scene,
when there is a lot of physical intimacy but

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:16.220
it's ultimately very emotionally empty, and
the scene that immediately follows it, when

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.810
there is not a serious physical interaction,
but there's this intense emotional connection.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.180
The argument here is that physical intimacy
can never stand in for emotional closeness.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.610
And that when teenagers attempt to conflate
these ideas, it inevitably fails.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.360
Hank, it doesn't take a deeply critical understanding
of literature to realise that Looking for

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:36.150
Alaska is arguing against vapid physical interactions,
not for them.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.459
Now Hank, some people are gonna say that kids
don't have the critical sophistication when

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:41.290
they're reading to understand that.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.849
And I have a message for those people.

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:45.610
Shut up and stop condescending to teenagers.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.819
Do you seriously think that teenagers aren't
able to read critically?

00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:52.379
When they read George Orwell's Animal Farm
do they head out to the pig farms to kill

00:02:52.379 --> 00:02:54.989
all the pigs because they're about to become
communist autocrats?

00:02:54.989 --> 00:02:58.650
When they read Huck Finn do they think that
Huck should turn Jim in because the demented

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.560
conscience of the community says so?

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.829
When they read Waiting for Godot do they think
that it's cool to just sit around and do nothing?

00:03:03.829 --> 00:03:06.760
Well, probably they do actually, that one
doesn't really work toward my point.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.760
Now obviously Hank, I don't think that a few
parents should get to decide what the kids

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.620
of other parents read in school.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:15.670
Those parents are qualified to sign or not
sign that permission slip as they see fit.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.989
And to speak frankly Hank, it also pisses
me off when small groups of well-organized

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:22.120
would-be book banners try to take over America's
public school systems.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.379
Now fortunately in Depew, there are a lot
of teachers and school administrators and

00:03:25.379 --> 00:03:29.220
school board members who are standing up for
my book and for the right of teachers to teach

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.049
it, and for the right of parents to choose
to have it taught to their kids.

00:03:33.049 --> 00:03:36.010
But Hank, sometimes these people who make
stands for intellectual freedom can feel like

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.270
they're going it alone.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.730
Which is why I'd like to ask from the bottom
of my heart that any nerdfighters that live

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:44.640
in or around Depew go to the school board
meeting on February 5th.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:45.970
(Info in the sidebar.)

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:49.360
Also, I've written a letter in defense of
the teachers who would like to teach my book.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.620
If you'd like to write a letter, send it to
sparksflyup at gmail dot com and I'll be sure

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:54.620
to forward it on.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.530
Hank, thanks to you and all the nerdfighters
in advance for your emails.

